|~ The streams a host on the command line installs: their topic,
   `::stdio`, the names of the streams under it, and the names it declares
   under the kinds of their subjects, `table` under `::vec` and `answer`
   under `::cell`. Their primitives take the values alone, the served
   subject and the values of their inputs, but that of `tap`, which reads
   the state of its call.
   The rules it holds: [QSHST#effect], [QSDSP#residence], [QSHST#cell]. ~|

:stdio
  |~~ The streams of the process of the host that installs them, each
      written by its address: `::stdio | in` reads standard input,
      `stdio/out` writes a line of standard output and `stdio/err` one of
      standard error, each answering its subject as it came, and
      `stdio/tap` shows a value halfway through a query on stderr as an
      answer prints. A query that writes to stdout by `stdio/out` holds the
      channel, and its answer is left unwritten. A value one query hands
      another over a pipe keeps its kinds as its literal, `print | stdio/out`
      writing it and `::stdio | in | parse | answer` reading it back.
      `table` draws a vector of maps as a text for a terminal, and a cell
      keeps an answer past the budget of every answer and a fact past the
      room of a fact, which `answer` reads back whole in a later query. The
      command that runs the host is the host's own, which its page tells,
      `::host | doc`.
      ::link~(::stdio/in) ::link~(::stdio/out) ::link~(::stdio/tap)
      ::link~(::vec/table) ::link~(::cell) ::link~(::qlang/hosts)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ No value: `::stdio` names the streams of the process. ~~| #[]
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::stdio` names the streams of the process, its page and its
            words, and takes no payload: it is asked by itself,
            `::stdio | doc`.
        ~~|
    | proves :declaresTable
        |~~ The streams declare `table` under vectors. ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/table)
    | proves :declaresTheStreams
        |~~ The streams declare their verbs under `::stdio`. ~~|
        ~(::stdio | manifest | has ::stdio/out)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAValue
        |~~ No value is put under `::stdio`. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag ::stdio !| tag | eq ::stdio/PayloadKindNotTakenError);

:stdio/err
  |~~ Writes a line to stderr, the channel of diagnostics, as `stdio/out`
      writes to stdout, and answers its subject as it came; the answer of
      the query is written as ever.
      ::snippet~([1 2 3] | stdio/err "counting" | count) writes `counting`
      to stderr and answers 3.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | on |~~ Any value, answered as it came. ~~| ::any
    | takes :text
        |~~ The text written, computed against the subject, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::string]
    | throws :TextKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The text `stdio/err` writes is a string: a value of another
            kind is made text first, `stdio/err json` or `stdio/err print`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The subject as it came. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `stdio/err` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line on
            stderr; another value is written as the text the call computes
            from it, `stdio/err json` or `stdio/err print`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `stdio/err` writes its subject, or the one text its call gives,
            `"hi" | stdio/err`; texts are joined first,
            `stdio/err (["a" "b"] | join "")`.
        ~~|
    | proves :TextKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A text that is no string is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | stdio/err 7 !| tag | eq ::stdio/err/TextKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :SubjectKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no line `stdio/err` writes alone. ~~|
        ~(42 | stdio/err !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | expected)] | eq [::stdio/err/SubjectKindNotTakenError #[::string]])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoTexts
        |~~ A call with two texts is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~("hi" | stdio/err "a" "b" !| tag | eq ::stdio/err/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:stdio/in
  |~~ Answers the text stdin holds, read to its end; a terminal with
      nothing piped answers the empty string. It is asked of `::stdio` at
      the head of a query, which reads the text as it came, the host
      lifting nothing into the subject, `::stdio | in | lines | count`
      counting the lines piped in and `::stdio | in | parseJson` reading
      the text as JSON alone. It takes no value.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | on |~~ The streams themselves, `::stdio`. ~~| ::tag
    | returns
        |~~ The text stdin held, the empty string when nothing was piped.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | impl ::builtin{};

:stdio/out
  |~~ Writes a line to stdout and answers its subject as it came: it
      writes the subject when it is a string, `"hi" | stdio/out`, or the
      text the call gives, computed against the subject,
      `42 | stdio/out json`, the steps after still reading `42`. A query
      that writes by `stdio/out` leaves its answer unwritten.
      ::snippet~([1 2] | stdio/out json | count) writes `[1,2]`.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | on |~~ Any value, answered as it came. ~~| ::any
    | takes :text
        |~~ The text written, computed against the subject, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::string]
    | throws :TextKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The text `stdio/out` writes is a string: a value of another
            kind is made text first, `stdio/out json` or `stdio/out print`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The subject as it came. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `stdio/out` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line;
            another value is written as the text the call computes from it,
            `42 | stdio/out json` or `{:id 7} | stdio/out print`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `stdio/out` writes its subject, or the one text its call gives,
            `"hi" | stdio/out`; texts are joined first,
            `stdio/out (["a" "b"] | join "")`.
        ~~|
    | proves :SubjectKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no line `stdio/out` writes alone. ~~|
        ~(42 | stdio/out !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | expected)] | eq [::stdio/out/SubjectKindNotTakenError #[::string]])
    | proves :TextKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no text to write. ~~|
        ~("hi" | stdio/out 5 !| tag | eq ::stdio/out/TextKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoTexts
        |~~ A call with two texts is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~("hi" | stdio/out "a" "b" !| tag | eq ::stdio/out/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:stdio/tap
  |~~ Writes `[tap <label>]` and its subject to stderr as an answer prints,
      within the budget of every answer, and answers the subject as it
      came, a look at a value halfway
      through a query:
      ::snippet~([{:on true} {:on false}] | stdio/tap :before | filter /on | stdio/tap :after | count)
      shows both vectors and answers 1. A subject past the budget is
      written as `::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError`, its `:answer` the cell
      that keeps the subject, which `answer` reads back whole, and
      `stdio/err print` writes a look whole.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | on |~~ Any value, answered as it came. ~~| ::any
    | takes :label
        |~~ The label written before the value, a keyword. ~~|
        /0 ::keyword
    | throws :LabelKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The label of `stdio/tap` is a keyword, `stdio/tap :before`,
            written before the value as `[tap before]`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The subject as it came. ~~| ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `stdio/tap` writes its subject under one label its call gives,
            `5 | stdio/tap :before`; a call with more labels is refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :LabelKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no label. ~~|
        ~(1 | stdio/tap "before" !| tag | eq ::stdio/tap/LabelKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoLabels
        |~~ A call with two labels is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | stdio/tap :before :after !| tag | eq ::stdio/tap/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:cell/answer
  |~~ Answers the value the host keeps under the cell, read back
      over the root and the names the query that kept it declared:
      `::cell"a3f9c2d1e04b" | answer | take 3` reads the first three
      elements of an answer that passed the budget of every answer, its
      refusal naming the cell as its `:answer`, and a fact of a failure past
      the room of a fact so. A host of the streams keeps it under
      `<tmp>/qlang/<root>/cells/<id>/`, `<id>` the hash of the query and what
      it keeps,
      the query, `source.qlang`, the values
      it declared, `names.qlang`, and the answer, `answer.qlang`, each as the
      core prints it, `<root>` the identity of the sources and the code it
      booted, so a cell kept over other sources is held by none, and a
      cell older than a day leaves when the host keeps another. A
      value under a kind the keeping query declared answers its names where
      the reading query declares that kind again, and `payload` in any
      query.
      ::link~(::cell) ::link~(::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The value the cell keeps. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectNotCellError
        |~~ `answer` reads a cell, `::cell"a3f9c2d1e04b" | answer`, the one
            the refusal of an answer past the budget names.
        ~~|
    | throws :SubjectNotHeldError
        |~~ The host holds no such cell: it aged past a day, the
            temporary folder was emptied, the cell was kept over other
            sources or by another host, or none drew it. The answer is read again by the query
            that made it, narrowed by `get`, `take` or `count` so it prints
            within the budget.
            ::link~(::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError)
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `answer` reads the whole a cell keeps and takes no value: a part
            of it is read after it, `answer | take 3`.
        ~~|
    | proves :SubjectNotHeldError/refusesACellNoneKept
        |~~ A cell no query kept is refused. ~~|
        ~(::cell"000000000000" | answer !| tag | eq ::cell/answer/SubjectNotHeldError)
    | proves :SubjectNotCellError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number names no cell. ~~|
        ~(5 | cell/answer !| tag | eq ::cell/answer/SubjectNotCellError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/table
  |~~ Answers a vector of maps drawn as a table of fixed-width columns, a
      text for a terminal. The columns are the keys in the order they
      first occur across the rows, and a key a row lacks draws an empty
      cell; a string cell prints bare, null as an empty cell, and every
      other value as its literal on one line, a doc with each line break
      of its prose written as a space. The empty vector draws as
      `(empty)`. A single map goes in a vector first, `get [~(it)] | table`, and
      the call takes no value.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The rows drawn as text, a line each. ~~| ::string
    | throws :SubjectNotVecError
        |~~ `table` draws a vector of maps; a single map goes in a vector
            first, `{:name "alice"} | get [~(it)] | table`.
        ~~|
    | throws :RowKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Every row of `table` is a map, its keys the columns; the path
            of its frame names the first that is not.
        ~~|
    | proves :drawsEachRow
        |~~ Each row is drawn. ~~|
        ~([{:name "alice" :age 30} {:name "bob" :age 25}] | table | contains "alice")
    | proves :drawsANestedValueAsItsLiteral
        |~~ A nested value is drawn as its literal. ~~|
        ~([{:at {:file "A.java" :line 12}}] | table | contains "{:file \"A.java\" :line 12}")
    | proves :drawsNothingAsEmpty
        |~~ An empty vector draws as `(empty)`. ~~|
        ~([] | table | eq "(empty)")
    | proves :drawsAMapInAVector
        |~~ A single map is drawn in a vector. ~~|
        ~({:name "alice"} | get [~(it)] | table | contains "alice")
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A single map is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~({:name "alice"} | table !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::map])
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no vector of rows. ~~|
        ~(42 | table !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::number])
    | proves :RowKindNotTakenError/refusesARowThatIsNoMap
        |~~ A row that is no map is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([42 "hello"] | table !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | found | get /0 | tag) ~(subject | expected)]
          | eq [::vec/table/RowKindNotTakenError /0 ::number #[::map]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

